CH = dict(num=2, title="Electricity: Circuits and Their Components", theme="Cell, lamp, switch, circuit, conductors, insulators",
intro="The chapter starts with a class trip to the Bhakra Nangal Dam and a list of the many uses of electricity. It then uses an ordinary torchlight to build the basic ideas: the electric cell and battery, incandescent and LED lamps, a complete electrical circuit, switches, circuit diagrams, and the difference between conductors and insulators.",
big_questions=["Why does a torch lamp glow only in one position of its switch?", "What is a complete electrical circuit?", "Why must an LED be connected a particular way?", "Why are wires made of metal and covered with plastic or rubber?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "3.0 Uses of electricity"),
("table", ["Use", "Examples"], [["Cooking", "Electric kettle, mixer-grinder, toaster, oven, microwave"], ["Lighting", "Homes, offices, streets, markets, factories"], ["Entertainment", "Television, radio"], ["Transportation", "Train, bus, car, scooter, lift, escalator"], ["Communication", "Mobile phone, Internet"], ["Heating and cooling", "Fan, room heater, immersion rod, geyser, refrigerator, air conditioner"], ["Others", "Water pump, crane, computer"]]),
("p", "Electricity is generated by windmills (wind energy), solar panels (Sun's energy), falling water and by burning natural gas or coal. It reaches homes and factories through wires. To study it safely we use a **portable source** such as a cell in a torch."),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Safety:** electricity is dangerous. Never experiment with mains supply or portable generators at home or school. Use only **cells or batteries** (as in torches, wall clocks, radios, remotes)."),
("h2", "3.1 A Torchlight"),
("p", "A torch has a **lamp**, a **switch** and two or more **electric cells** inside. In one position of the switch the lamp glows, in the other it does not. To understand why, we study each component."),
("h2", "3.2 A Simple Electrical Circuit"),
("h3", "3.2.1 Electric cell"),
("ul", ["An electric cell has two **terminals**: positive (+ve) and negative (–ve).", "The small **metal cap** is the **positive** terminal; the flat **metal disc** is the **negative** terminal.", "A cell is a **portable source of electrical energy**."]),
("h3", "3.2.2 Battery"),
("ul", ["In a torch with two cells, the lamp glows only when the cells are placed in the correct order: the **positive terminal of one cell touches the negative terminal of the next**.", "Two or more cells connected like this form a **battery**.", "More cells supply energy for a **longer time** and/or **more energy**.", "In everyday speech even a single cell (e.g. in a mobile phone) is called a battery.", "In devices with cells side by side, a thick wire or metal strip joins the + of one cell to the – of the next; the '+' and '–' signs are printed inside the compartment."]),
("h3", "3.2.3 Electric lamp"),
("table", ["Feature", "Incandescent lamp", "LED lamp"], [["Light-producing part", "Thin wire called **filament**, which gets hot and glows", "No filament; a light emitting diode"], ["Terminals", "Two terminals: metal tip at centre and metal case; they do not touch each other", "Two terminals: **longer wire = positive**, **shorter wire = negative**"], ["Which way to connect?", "**Either way**; polarity does not matter", "**Only one way**; current passes in one direction only"], ["Where used", "Old torches and bulbs", "Most modern torches; may use one or more LEDs"]]),
("ul", ["In an incandescent lamp the filament is attached to **two thicker supporting wires**: one goes to the metal case at the base and the other to the metal tip at the centre.", "A lamp is said to be **fused** when its filament breaks; a broken filament stops current, so the lamp will not glow even when connected to a cell."]),
("h3", "3.2.4 Making a lamp glow (Activity 3.6)"),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Gather", "Cell, torch lamp, cell holder, lamp holder and four lengths of wire."), ("Step 2 — Strip wires", "Remove about 1 cm of plastic covering from both ends of each wire to expose the metal."), ("Step 3 — Fit the cell", "Fix wires to the cell holder and put the cell with its negative terminal towards the spring side. Without a holder, use electrical tape."), ("Step 4 — Fit the lamp", "Attach two wires to the lamp holder screws (or tape them to the lamp ends)."), ("Step 5 — Predict, then observe", "In the book's six arrangements the lamp glows only in **arrangements 1 and 6**, where each lamp terminal is connected to a different terminal of the cell.")]),
("h3", "3.2.5 An electrical circuit"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "An **electrical circuit** is a complete path for electric current formed when one terminal of the lamp is joined to one terminal of the cell and the other lamp terminal to the other cell terminal. The lamp glows **only when current passes** through the circuit."),
("ul", ["By convention, the direction of current is **from the positive to the negative terminal** of the cell.", "In an incandescent lamp the current passes through the **filament**, which makes it glow."]),
("h3", "Making an LED glow (Activity 3.7)"),
("ul", ["Use two cells in a holder (negative terminal towards the spring) and connect the **positive of the battery to the longer wire** of the LED and the negative to the shorter wire. The LED glows.", "Interchange the connections: the LED **does not glow**, because current passes through it in one direction only.", "The positive terminal of a battery made of two cells is the holder terminal connected to the + terminal of one cell; the other is connected to the – of the other cell."]),
("h3", "3.2.6 Electric switch"),
("steps", [("Make a switch (Activity 3.8)", "Fix a drawing pin through the ring of a safety pin on a cardboard so it can rotate; fix a second drawing pin so the free end of the safety pin can touch it; connect a wire to each pin."), ("Test it (Activity 3.9)", "Connect cell, lamp and switch. With the pin not touching the second drawing pin the lamp is **off**; when it touches both pins the lamp glows.")]),
("table", ["Switch position", "Circuit state", "Current", "Lamp"], [["ON", "**Closed** (complete)", "Flows from + to – of the cell", "Glows"], ["OFF", "**Open** (air gap)", "Does not flow", "Does not glow"]]),
("p", "A **switch** is a simple device that completes or breaks a circuit. It can be placed **anywhere** in the circuit. House switches work the same way though they look different."),
("h2", "3.3 Circuit Diagrams"),
("table", ["Component", "How its symbol looks / what it means"], [["Electric cell", "Two parallel lines: **long line = positive**, **short thick line = negative**"], ["Battery", "Two or more cell symbols joined"], ["Electric lamp", "A circle with a cross inside"], ["LED", "A triangle pointing in the direction current can flow, with two outward arrows showing that it **emits light**"], ["Switch ON / OFF", "A closed or open gap in the wire"], ["Wire", "A straight line"]]),
("p", "A representation of a circuit using symbols is a **circuit diagram**. Drawing circuits as diagrams makes them quick to draw and easy to read. International bodies such as the **IEC, ANSI and IEEE** standardise symbols so people everywhere understand the same diagram."),
("h2", "3.4 Electrical Conductors and Insulators"),
("steps", [("Make a tester (Activity 3.11)", "Connect a cell and lamp with two free wire ends. Touching the free ends together makes the lamp glow, so the tester works."), ("Test objects", "Touch the free ends to both ends of each object (spoon, coin, cork, rubber, glass, key, pin, plastic scale, wooden block, aluminium foil, candle, needle, cardboard, paper, pencil lead). Make sure the wires do not touch each other.")]),
("table", ["Conductors (lamp glows)", "Insulators (lamp does not glow)"], [["Metal key, spoon, coin, pin, needle, aluminium foil, pencil lead (graphite)", "Wood, plastic scale, glass bangle, paper, wax candle, rubber eraser, cork, cardboard"]]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Conductors (good conductors)** allow electric current to flow easily. **Insulators (poor conductors)** do not allow current to pass through them."),
("ul", ["Metals are conductors. **Silver, copper and gold** are the best conductors, but **copper** is used for wires because it is cheaper and plentiful.", "Plastic, rubber and ceramics are **insulators**; they cover wires, plug tops and switches to protect us from shocks.", "Wires, switch parts, plug connectors and sockets are **conductors**; their coverings are **insulators**: both are important."]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Our body is a conductor.** Current through the body can cause severe injury or death. Never touch switches or plugs with wet hands, never use electrical devices in wet places and never handle damaged wires or broken plugs."),
("note", "NOTE", "**DC and AC:** batteries give **Direct Current (DC)** and power small devices. Power plants supply **Alternating Current (AC)** to wall sockets, and it can run larger appliances."),
("p", "**Science and society:** cells come in different forms: cylindrical cells for torches, clocks, remotes and toys; button cells for watches and hearing aids; rechargeable batteries for phones, laptops and electric vehicles."),
("h2", "Reasoning questions from the chapter"),
("qa", [("Why does a torch lamp glow in only one position of the switch?", "In the ON position the circuit is complete and current flows through the lamp; in the OFF position there is an air gap."),
 ("How are two cells joined in a battery?", "Positive terminal of one cell touches the negative terminal of the next."),
 ("Why does the LED not glow when its wires are interchanged?", "Current passes through an LED in one direction only; the longer wire must connect to the positive terminal."),
 ("Will a lamp glow if the insulation is not stripped from the wire ends?", "No: the insulating cover blocks the contact, so no complete circuit forms."),
 ("If one filament in a circuit is broken, will the other lamp glow?", "In a single series loop, no, because the circuit is broken; if the lamps are on separate paths, the other still glows."),
 ("How can you find the terminals of a battery whose signs have faded?", "Connect it to an LED: it glows when the longer wire is joined to the positive terminal; this identifies the terminals."),
 ("Why are wires made of copper and covered with plastic?", "Copper is a good, cheap conductor; plastic is an insulator that protects against shock and short contact."),
 ("What is a circuit diagram?", "A drawing of a circuit using standard symbols."),
 ("Give four reasons why a lamp may not glow when connected.", "Fused filament, loose connection, dead cell, wrong terminals/LED reversed, open switch, broken wire."),
 ("State the direction of current in a circuit.", "From the positive terminal to the negative terminal of the cell, through the circuit."),
 ("Differentiate DC and AC.", "DC flows in one direction (batteries, small devices); AC (wall socket) is used for larger appliances.")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["List the actions you could not do in your daily life if power failed for two days.", "Run a toy fan with a solar panel.", "Visit an electrical shop to list types of cells and their uses.", "Classify home objects as insulators only, conductors only, or both."]),
],
takeaways=["A **cell** has + (metal cap) and – (flat disc) terminals; two or more cells joined + to – form a **battery**.", "An **incandescent lamp** glows by a hot **filament**; an **LED** has no filament and works only when connected the correct way (**longer wire to +**).",
 "A lamp glows only in a **closed circuit**; current flows from **+ to –**.", "A **switch** closes (ON) or opens (OFF) the circuit.", "A **circuit diagram** uses standard symbols.", "**Conductors** (metals, copper best for wires) let current pass; **insulators** (plastic, rubber, glass, wood) do not.", "The human body conducts electricity: handle electricity with great care."])
